{"cuisine/chinese-latin-american-cuisine-cuba":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/chinese-latin-american-cuisine-cuba","n":"Cuban Chinese cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Latin American Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-latin-american-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Cuba","intro":[["Chinese Latin American or Chino-Latino cuisine, associated with Asian Latin Americans of Chinese origin, combines elements of Chinese cuisine with other Latin American influences. It is found in Chinese communities and Chinatowns across Latin America, including Peru (where it is known as chifa) and Cuba. It has spread to the United States with the migration of Asian Latin Americans, particularly the migration of Chinese Cubans to New York City."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"chinese-latin-american-cuisine-cuba"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_Latin_American_cuisine#Cuba"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Latin American Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-latin-american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Latin American Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-latin-american-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas"},"cuisine/sikkimese-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/sikkimese-cuisine","n":"Sikkimese cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["North East Indian cuisine","cuisine/north-east-indian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in India","intro":[["Sikkimese cuisine is a collection of dishes representative of the Indigenous peoples of Sikkim. Rice is the staple food. Millet and fermented foods traditionally constituted a significant portion of the cuisine."],["Nepalese cuisine is popular, as Sikkim is the only state of India with an ethnic Nepali majority. Many restaurants in Sikkim serve various types of Nepalese cuisine, such as the Limbu, Newa and Thakali cuisines. Tibetan cuisine has also influenced Sikkimese cuisine."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"sikkimese-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sikkimese_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Taste of Sikkim","ref":"site/tasteofsikkim-blogspot-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"tasteofsikkim.blogspot.com","note":"Blog dedicated to Sikkimese home recipes.","url":"https://tasteofsikkim.blogspot.com","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["North East Indian cuisine","cuisine/north-east-indian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"cuisine/kodava-people":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/kodava-people","n":"Kodava cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Karnataka cuisine","cuisine/karnataka-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in India","intro":[["The Kodavas (Codavas or Kodagas), also called Coorgs, are an endogamous Dravidian ethnolinguistic group from the region of Kodagu in the southern Indian state of Karnataka, who natively speak the Kodava language."],["Kodavas worship ancestors, nature, and weapons such as swords, bows, arrows, and later guns."],["They are traditionally land-owning agriculturists and patrilineal, with martial customs. Originally small landholders, they gained relative prosperity with the advent of coffee cultivation in the nineteenth and twentieth centuries."],["The Kodava tribe forms the single largest caste in the district of Kodagu; they are reportedly over 30% of Kodagu's Hindu population, and play a major role in deciding the political candidates and winners there. The Kodava tribe also forms more than 60 percent of the Kodava-speaking population."],["Kodavas are the only ethnic group in India permitted to carry firearms without a license."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"kodava-people"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kodava_people"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Sailu's Kitchen (Kodava cuisine)","ref":"site/sailusfood-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"sailusfood.com","note":"Overview of Kodava (Coorgi) cooking with recipes on Sailaja's Indian food blog.","url":"https://www.sailusfood.com/indian-food-kodava-cuisine-coorgi-recipes","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Karnataka cuisine","cuisine/karnataka-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Within Kodava cooking","count":1,"links":[["Mangalurean Catholic cuisine","cuisine/mangalorean-catholic-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"cuisine/lombard-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/lombard-cuisine","n":"Lombard cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Italy","intro":[["Lombard cuisine consists of the cooking traditions and practices of the Lombardy region. The historical events of its provinces and of the diversity of its territories resulted in a varied culinary tradition. First courses in Lombard cuisine range from risottos to soups and stuffed pasta (in broth or not), and a large choice of second-course meat or fish dishes, due to the many lakes and rivers of Lombardy."],["The cuisine of the various Lombardy provinces have the following traits in common: prevalence of rice and stuffed pasta over dry pasta, both butter and olive oil for cooking, dishes cooked for a long time, as well as the widespread use of pork, milk and dairy products, and egg-based preparations, as well as the consumption of polenta, common to the whole of northern Italy."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"lombard-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lombard_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Great Italian Chefs: Abruzzese recipes","ref":"site/greatitalianchefs-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"greatitalianchefs.com","note":"Collection of Lombard dishes from Italian chefs on an established recipe site.","url":"https://greatitalianchefs.com/collections/lombardian-recipes","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":2,"links":[["Gremolata","dish/gremolata"],["Pan meino","dish/pan-meino"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Lemon zest","ingredient/lemon-zest"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Within Lombard cooking","count":1,"links":[["Mantuan cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-mantua"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine"},"cuisine/garifuna-foodways":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/garifuna-foodways","n":"Garifuna foodways","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]],"sub":"Indigenous foodways, Caribbean coast of Honduras, Belize, Guatemala and Nicaragua, and New York City","names":"Garinagu, Garifuna","intro":[["The Garinagu were exiled from St. Vincent and settled along the Caribbean coast of Central America, and the largest Garifuna community outside Central America is in New York City, mostly in the South Bronx. Their kitchen runs on ",["Cassava","ingredient/cassava"],", green and ripe ",["plantains","ingredient/plantain-cooking"],", green bananas, ",["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"]," and fish."],[["Ereba","dish/casabe"],", cassava bread, is women's work that takes days: the roots are peeled, grated and strained, and the flour is spread in thin rounds on a hot griddle, then shared with family and neighbors. Its color is the yellow stripe on the Garifuna flag. ",["Hudut","dish/hudut"]," is plantain pounded smooth and served with falmo, fish soup made with coconut milk. Darasa are ",["tamales","dish/tamale"]," made with green banana instead of corn, and durudia are thick tortillas made with coconut milk."]],"src":"note","sources":[{"name":"Remezcla, New York City's Garifuna community preserves its rich culture through food","url":"https://remezcla.com/features/culture/garifuna-food-culture-new-york-city/"},{"name":"Warasa Garifuna Drum School, Garifuna food","url":"https://www.warasadrumschool.com/garifuna-culture/garifuna-food"}],"actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"garifuna-foodways"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Dishes","count":3,"links":[["Casabe","dish/casabe"],["Hudut","dish/hudut"],["Tamale","dish/tamale"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Cassava","ingredient/cassava"],["Plantain (cooking)","ingredient/plantain-cooking"],["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"],["Fish","ingredient/fish"],["Banana leaf","ingredient/banana-leaf"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Corn husks","ingredient/corn-husks"],["Cornmeal","ingredient/cornmeal"],["Maize","ingredient/maize"],["Masa","ingredient/masa"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas"},"dish/amigos-restaurant-chain":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/amigos-restaurant-chain","n":"Amigos (restaurant chain)","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Mexican cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Amigos is a fast-food Mexican restaurant chain founded in Lincoln, Nebraska. The first location opened at 5500 'O' Street on June 17, 1980. Currently, there are 30 Amigos restaurants in multiple cities. Amigos is one of two regional restaurant chains that serves \"Cheese Frenchees\", a regional delicacy created by the Lincoln-based chain King's Food Host in the 1950s."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-amigos-restaurant-chain"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amigos_(restaurant_chain)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Mexican cuisine","cuisine/mexican-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Mexico in My Kitchen","ref":"site/mexicoinmykitchen-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"mexicoinmykitchen.com","note":"Mely Martínez's long-running blog of Mexican home-style recipes.","ur