ietnamese-American Huy Vu's recipes for home-style Vietnamese dishes.","url":"https://www.hungryhuy.com/vietnamese/","suggested":true}]},{"h":"More from Vietnamese cuisine","links":[["Alkemē","dish/alkeme"],["Bánh bao","dish/banh-bao"],["Bánh bột lọc","dish/banh-bot-loc"],["Bánh giầy","dish/banh-giay"],["Bánh tráng","dish/banh-trang"],["Chả rươi","dish/cha-ruoi"],["Chả trứng","dish/cha-trung"],["Chạo tôm","dish/chao-tom"],["Coconut candy","dish/coconut-candy"],["Cốm","dish/com"],["Ha VL","dish/ha-vl"],["Hanoi Kitchen","dish/hanoi-kitchen"],["Indochine (restaurant)","dish/indochine-restaurant"],["Lee's Sandwiches","dish/lee-s-sandwiches"],["Lúc Lắc Vietnamese Kitchen","dish/luc-lac-vietnamese-kitchen"],["Mắm (restaurant)","dish/mam-restaurant"],["Mắm nêm","dish/mam-nem"],["Mama Đút","dish/mama-ut"],["Matta (restaurant)","dish/matta-restaurant"],["Nước chấm","dish/nuoc-cham"],["Ốc vú nàng","dish/oc-vu-nang"],["Phở Hòa","dish/pho-hoa"],["Phở Kim","dish/pho-kim"],["Pho Oregon","dish/pho-oregon"],["Rose VL Deli","dish/rose-vl-deli"],["Shaking beef","dish/shaking-beef"],["Tamarind Tree (restaurant)","dish/tamarind-tree-restaurant"],["The Black Sheep (restaurant)","dish/the-black-sheep-restaurant"],["Tương","dish/tuong"]]}]},"dish/lupis-food":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/lupis-food","n":"Lupis (food)","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Indonesian cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Lupis (sometimes lopis) is an Indonesian traditional sweet cake made of glutinous rice, banana leaves, coconut, and brown sugar sauce. Lupis is one of many glutinous rice desserts from Indonesia. Lupis are sometimes cylindrically shaped like Lontong, or shaped like a triangle. Lupis is usually eaten with thick palm sugar syrup and with shredded coconut toppings. Often eaten at breakfast or as a side dish during the evening, lupis is often sold at traditional marketplaces throughout Indonesia and is a popular food found nationwide, but especially in middle and eastern Java as well as West Sumatra. Lupis is one of the top desserts that tourists who visit Purwokerto in Java seek."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-lupis-food"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lupis_(food)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":5,"links":[["Banana leaf","ingredient/banana-leaf"],["Coconut","ingredient/coconut"],["Glutinous rice","ingredient/glutinous-rice"],["Palm sugar","ingredient/palm-sugar"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Daily Cooking Quest","ref":"site/dailycookingquest-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"dailycookingquest.com","note":"Indonesian home cook Anita's blog with a large archive of Indonesian recipes.","url":"https://dailycookingquest.com/cuisines/indonesian.html","suggested":true}]},{"h":"More from Indonesian cuisine","links":[["Arem-arem","dish/arem-arem"],["Arsik","dish/arsik"],["Asam pedas","dish/asam-pedas"],["Ayam goreng","dish/ayam-goreng"],["Ayam goreng kalasan","dish/ayam-goreng-kalasan"],["Ayam panggang","dish/ayam-panggang"],["Ayam Taliwang","dish/ayam-taliwang"],["Bajigur","dish/bajigur"],["Bakakak hayam","dish/bakakak-hayam"],["Bakpia","dish/bakpia"],["Bakwan","dish/bakwan"],["Bekasang","dish/bekasang"],["Betutu","dish/betutu"],["Bika ambon","dish/bika-ambon"],["Binte biluhuta","dish/binte-biluhuta"],["Bir pletok","dish/bir-pletok"],["Bolu kukus","dish/bolu-kukus"],["Botok","dish/botok"],["Brem","dish/brem"],["Bubur kacang hijau","dish/bubur-kacang-hijau"],["Bubur ketan hitam","dish/bubur-ketan-hitam"],["Bubur pedas","dish/bubur-pedas"],["Buntil","dish/buntil"],["Burgo (food)","dish/burgo-food"],["Cendol","dish/cendol"],["Cenil","dish/cenil"],["Cilok","dish/cilok"],["Cimpa","dish/cimpa"],["Corn fritter","dish/corn-fritter"],["Coto makassar","dish/coto-makassar"],["Dadar gulung","dish/dadar-gulung"],["Dadiah","dish/dadiah"],["Dengke mas naniura","dish/dengke-mas-naniura"],["Empal gentong","dish/empal-gentong"],["Emping","dish/emping"],["Es buah","dish/es-buah"],["Es doger","dish/es-doger"],["Es goyobod","dish/es-goyobod"],["Gado-gado","dish/gado-gado"],["Geplak","dish/geplak"]]}]},"ingredient/marinade":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/marinade","n":"Marinade","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Marinating is the process of soaking foods in a seasoned, often acidic, liquid before cooking. This sauce, called the marinade, can be either acidic (made with ingredients such as vinegar, lemon juice, or wine), or enzymatic (made with ingredients such as pineapple, papaya, yogurt, or ginger), or has a neutral pH. In addition to these ingredients, a marinade often contains oil, salt, herbs, and spices to further flavor the food items. It is commonly used to flavor foods and to tenderize tougher cuts of meat; the process may last seconds or days."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-marinade"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marinade"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Korean cuisine","cuisine/korean-cuisine",[["Meat jun","dish/meat-jun"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Egg wash","ingredient/egg-wash"],["Soy sauce","ingredient/soy-sauce"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]}]},"ingredient/tabasco-sauce":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/tabasco-sauce","n":"Tabasco sauce","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Tabasco is an American brand of hot sauce made from vinegar, tabasco peppers, and salt. It is produced by the McIlhenny Company of Avery Island in southern Louisiana, having been created in 1868 by Edmund McIlhenny. Originally, the tabasco peppers were grown only on Avery Island; they are now primarily cultivated in Central America, South America, and Africa. The Tabasco sauce brand also has multiple varieties, including the original red sauce, habanero, jalapeño, chipotle, sriracha, and scorpion. Tabasco products are sold in more than 195 countries and territories, and packaged in 36 languages and dialects."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-tabasco-sauce"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tabasco_sauce"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Yoshoku","cuisine/yoshoku",[["Naporitan","dish/naporitan"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Agaricus bisporus","ingredient/agaricus-bisporus"],["Bacon","ingredient/bacon"],["Ketchup","ingredient/ketchup"],["Sausage","ingredient/sausage"],["Spaghetti","ingredient/spaghetti"]]}]},"ingredient/nile-tilapia":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/nile-tilapia","n":"Nile tilapia","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["The Nile tilapia (Oreochromis niloticus) is a species of tilapia, a cichlid occurring naturally in parts of Africa (such as its namesake Nile River) and the Levant, though numerous introduced populations exist outside its natural range. This current wide range is caused by its high commercial value as a food fish, where it is marketed as mango fish (not to be confused with the mango tilapia, or Sarotherodon galilaeus), nilotica, or boulti, along with many other names, both local and foreign. Due to its value, the Nile tilapia is widely aquacultured across the world due to its hardiness and a mode of reproduction conducive to mass rearing, namely mouthbrooding, and various attempts have been made to increase production yields, including hybridization with other tilapias."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-nile-tilapia"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nile_tilapia"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Natinombur","dish/natinombur"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Andaliman","ingredient/andaliman"],["Bird's eye chili","ingredient/bird-s-eye-chili"],["Candlenut","ingredient/candlenut"],["Etlingera elatior","ingredient/etlingera-elatior"],["Galangal","ingredient/galangal"],["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Lemongrass","ingredient/lemongrass"],["Orange","ingredient/orange"]]}]},"ingredient/maraschino":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/maraschino","n":"Maraschino","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Maraschino is a liqueur obtained from the distillation of Marasca cherries. The small, slightly sour fruit of the Marasca cherry tree (Prunus cerasus var. marasca), which grows wild along parts of the Dalmatian coast in Croatia, lends the liqueur its unique aroma."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-maraschino"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maraschino"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine",[["Nesselrode pudding","dish/nesselrode-pudding"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Currant","ingredient/currant"],["Egg yolk","ingredient/egg-yolk"],["Powdered sugar","ingredient/powdered-sugar"],["Sponge cake","ingredient/sponge-cake"],["Succade","ingredient/succade"],["Whipped cream","ingredient/whipped-cream"]]}]},"dish/pancho-villa-restaurant":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/pancho-villa-restaurant","n":"Pancho Villa (restaurant)","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Tex-Mex cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Pancho Villa is a Finnish restaurant franchise serving Tex Mex cuisine. The first restaurant was opened in 2001 in Tammela, Tampere."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-pancho-villa-restaurant"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pancho_Villa_(restaurant)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Te